## Reflection

This was one of the most complex debugging sessions I've had. What started as "remove mock data" turned into a complete reconstruction of how the recommendation system integrates historical data, handles search queries, and processes crop name variations.

The most valuable lesson was about the importance of data integrity in agricultural systems. The difference between using 0mm (current) rainfall and 660mm (seasonal average) rainfall completely changes which crops are recommended. A system using current conditions might recommend drought-tolerant crops during a dry week, even though the rainy season will bring abundant water. This could lead farmers to make poor planting decisions that reduce yields or cause crop failure.

The beans search issue was particularly instructive. The system was technically working—it was ranking beans correctly as #6—but the arbitrary top-3 limit made that ranking invisible to users. This reminded me that a system can be internally correct but still fail users if it doesn't expose the right information at the right time.

Finally, the experience of dealing with accumulated technical debt (the duplicate code, syntax errors, and malformed sections) reinforced the importance of clean code practices. Each failed automated fix added more cruft, making the problem worse. The user's manual intervention fixed in seconds what I struggled with for many iterations. Sometimes the solution isn't more sophisticated automation, but rather recognizing when to step back and take a different approach.

The system is now production-ready for its core use case: providing Malawian farmers with accurate, data-driven crop recommendations for rainy season planting based on real historical weather patterns and proven agricultural science. That's a meaningful achievement.
